// `\\` is a newline, as `¶` is: at the end of a `>>` and as a statement of its own.
//
// The web interpreter accepted it only at the end of a `>>` and refused the second
// line below with "expected expression, found '\\'" (BUG-VMJS-003, found by the
// JavaScript port of the VM, whose AST is compared against Rust's node for node).

>> "a" \\
\\
>> "b" ¶
